=== layout: bookend

# Learning Objectives

@body

- Distinguish allele, genotype, and phenotype frequencies.
- Count alleles from diploid genotype data.
- Calculate allele frequencies from population counts.
- Apply allele counting to the CCR5 and blood-group examples.

=== layout: section

# Allele Frequencies

=== layout: one-panel

# *Definitions*

@body

- [*Gene pool*]{underline=true} &#8211; Includes all alleles present in the population - total aggregate of genes
- [*Phenotype frequency*]{underline=true} &#8211; proportion of individuals in a population that are a particular phenotype
  - example\: population of flowers ..Red and white
- [*Genotype frequency*]{underline=true} &#8211; proportion of individuals in a population that are a particular genotype.
  - example\: Among flowers &#8211; RR, Rr and rr

=== layout: one-panel

# *Gene Pools and Allele Frequencies*

@body

- A [*population*]{underline=true} is a localized group of individuals capable of interbreeding and producing fertile offspring
- A [*gene pool*]{underline=true} consists of all the alleles for all loci in a population
- A locus is [*fixed*]{underline=true} if all individuals in a population are homozygous for the same allele

=== layout: one-panel

# *Definitions*

@body

- [*Allele frequency*]{underline=true} &#8211; proportion of all the alleles that are of specified type
  - example\: allele frequency of R & allele frequency of  r
- [*Evolution*]{underline=true} &#8211; rate of change in frequency of alleles in a population
  - *Natural Selection* &#8211; differential success in the reproduction of different phenotypes resulting from the interaction of organisms with their environment.
  - Evolution occurs when natural selection (or other factors) causes changes in relative frequencies of alleles in the gene pool.

=== layout: one-panel

# *Allele Frequency*

@body

- The frequency of an allele in a population can be calculated
  - For diploid organisms, the total number of alleles at a locus is the total number of individuals times 2
  - The total number of dominant alleles at a locus is two alleles for each homozygous dominant individual plus one allele for each heterozygous individual; the same logic applies for recessive alleles

=== layout: one-panel

# *Population Genetics*

@body

- Population genetics studies groups of individuals and includes studies of
  - Allelic variation among individuals
  - Transmission of allelic variants from parents to offspring generation after generation
  - Temporal changes in the genetic makeup of a population due to systematic and random evolutionary forces

=== layout: section

# CCR5 gene

=== layout: one-plus-two-panels

# *AIDS-resistance phenotype*

@left

- Chemokine receptor gene (CCR5) found in human population
- CCR5 receptor enables HIV virus infect CD4 class of T cells in immune system
  - it is necessary for HIV infection to progress.
- 10% humans contain CCR5 one allele known as &#916;32
  - 32 bps deletion within coding region
  - nonfunctional receptor

@top-right

![Slide image 1](assets/lect14b-allele_freq/ccr5_receptors.drawn.svg)

@bottom-right

- Individuals homozygous (&#916;32/&#916;32) do not have functional CCR5 receptors and are resistant to HIV-1 infection
- Heterozygotes have 2-3-year-slower progression to AIDS relative to wild types

=== layout: one-panel

