@body

![Slide image 7](assets/lect14b-allele_freq/image_006.png)

![Slide image 7](assets/lect14b-allele_freq/image_007.png)

- A1 = 2 + 2 + 5 + 1 = 10
- A2 = 5 + 12 + 12 + 10 = 39
- A3 = 10 + 5 + 5 + 1 = 21
- Total alleles = 70
- f(A1) = 10/70 = 0.14
- f(A2) = 39/70 = 0.56
- f(A3) = 21/70 = 0.30

=== layout: one-panel

# *Estimating Allele Frequencies*

@body

- When directly counting alleles is not possible due to the presence of a dominant allele, a different method must be used.
  - because dominant homozygotes would be identical to heterozygotes
  - Though if the gene is X-linked, allele can easily be counted in males.
- When the second most frequent allele of a gene has a frequency greater than 0.01, the gene is [*polymorphic*]{color=#339966}.

=== layout: one-panel

# *Theory of Allele Frequencies*

@body

- [When the members of a population mate randomly, it is easy to predict the frequencies of the genotypes from the frequencies of their constituent alleles.]{color=#339966}
- [Because of this we can make guesses on frequencies in the next section]{color=#339966}

=== layout: bookend

# Key Takeaways

@body

- Diploid individuals contribute two alleles at a locus.
- Count both alleles in each heterozygote.
- Allele frequencies sum to one.
- Genotype frequency and allele frequency are different.

